adjudication.rationale:
In `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts` the secret check is:

```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET)
  return 403;
```

If `CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is not set, `process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is `undefined`. If the request also omits the header (common for unauthenticated requests), `req.headers["calcom-webhook-secret"]` is also `undefined`. The comparison becomes `undefined !== undefined` which is `false`, so the code does **not** return 403 and the request passes the secret check. This is a real auth bypass when the env var is unset.

The handler gates access with:

```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET) {
  return res.status(403)
}
```

If `APP_CREDENTIAL_SHARING_ENABLED` is true and `process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is *unset* (i.e., `undefined`), then an unauthenticated request that omits the webhook header will yield `req.headers["calcom-webhook-secret"] === undefined`. The comparison becomes `undefined !== undefined`, which is `false`, so the request is treated as authorized and proceeds to update/create credentials for the supplied `userId`/`appSlug`. This is a real auth bypass when the secret is not configured.

In apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts the auth check is:

if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET) { ...403 }

If CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET is not set in the environment (i.e., undefined) and the request omits the header (so req.headers[...] is also undefined), the comparison becomes `undefined !== undefined` which is false, so the request is incorrectly accepted. This is a real fail-open authentication bug introduced by the diff (the new webhook endpoint). The finding’s mention of missing test is ancillary, but the underlying bypass is verifiable from the code.
